Done. Compiles cleanly and the test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).

## What I fixed

The reviewer's report was a raw copy-paste-detector dump: the vast majority of its ~130 "duplicate block" lines are false positives — Apache license headers matching across every file (`SwaggerConfig.java:1-17 <=> …`), and the structurally-similar-by-design Spring repository/controller/exception classes. None of those are code I introduced, so I left them alone.

The one genuine finding, anchored at **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:126`**, was real: the refactor rewrote `getOwner` to load the `Owner` entity (so it could compute the derived `bulkSignupWarning` field), which made its opening block byte-for-byte identical to `deleteOwner` (the `121-126 <=> 185-190` pair in the report):

```java
Owner owner = this.clinicService.findOwnerById(ownerId);
if (owner == null) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
}
```

That same "find the owner or return 404" preamble was copied verbatim into `getOwner`, `updateOwner`, `deleteOwner`, and `addPetToOwner`.

I consolidated it into a single generic helper and routed all four endpoints through it:

```java
private <T> ResponseEntity<T> withOwner(Integer ownerId, Function<Owner, ResponseEntity<T>> action) {
    Owner owner = this.clinicService.findOwnerById(ownerId);
    if (owner == null) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }
    return action.apply(owner);
}
```

Behavior is unchanged — same lookup, same 404, same success responses/status codes, and `deleteOwner` keeps its method-level `@Transactional`. I deliberately left `getOwnersPet`/`updateOwnersPet` alone: they use a different nested `!= null` shape (the trailing 404 also covers the *pet*-not-found case), so they aren't the flagged exact-duplicate block and rewriting them would risk a subtle control-flow change.

This is the preparation step only — I did not implement the upcoming `identityKey` duplicate-detection consolidation hinted at in the test.

